  "query_preview": "/Monday through Thursday/... \nStrong, expansive ridging will remain nearly in place next week \nacross the southwest and central United States with the anti-\ncyclone center rotating back from central Colorado into New Mexico\nand Arizona. Early in the week, sufficient easterly flow will \nprevail over the region juxtaposed with marginally supportive \nmixing ratios of 9-11 g/kg for scattered storms. There remains \nconsiderable uncertainty regarding a more preferred time frame for\nhigher POPS in any given location with mesoscale details likely \nbecoming more important towards convective evolution. \nUnfortunately, the mandated NBM/WPC forecast during this time only\nfurther complicates and diminishes the forecast confidence with \nnumerous instances of spurious QPF forcing procedural, un-\nscientific higher POPs spread over uncommon time periods based on \nclimatology and the preponderance of trusted model output. In \nreality, this appears to be a very typical monsoon pattern with \nalternating convectively active and inactive days. By the latter \nhalf of the week, there are a handful of NAEFS members indicating \nnotable strengthening of the high pressure with 600dm H5 heights \nmaterializing over northern AZ/southern UT. It appears the NBM/WPC\nforecasts are latching onto this scenario as POPs dwindle to much\nlower than expected values given the ensemble mean synoptic \npattern. The larger impact of this outcome would be a return to \nmajor HeatRisk and extreme heat thresholds towards the end of the \nweek.",
